    1.          INTEGER FUNCTION I1MACH(I)
    2.    C***BEGIN PROLOGUE  I1MACH
    3.    C***DATE WRITTEN   750101   (YYMMDD)
    4.    C***REVISION DATE  840405   (YYMMDD)
    5.    C***CATEGORY NO.  R1
    6.    C***KEYWORDS  MACHINE CONSTANTS
    7.    C***AUTHOR  FOX, P. A., (BELL LABS)
    8.    C           HALL, A. D., (BELL LABS)
    9.    C           SCHRYER, N. L., (BELL LABS)
   10.    C***PURPOSE  Returns integer machine dependent constants
   11.    C***DESCRIPTION
   12.    C
   13.    C * * * * * * * * * * * * * * * * * * * * * * * * * * * * *
   14.    C   These machine constant routines must be activated for
   15.    C   a particular environment.
   16.    C * * * * * * * * * * * * * * * * * * * * * * * * * * * * *
   17.    C
   18.    C     I1MACH can be used to obtain machine-dependent parameters
   19.    C     for the local machine environment.  It is a function
   20.    C     subroutine with one (input) argument, and can be called
   21.    C     as follows, for example
   22.    C
   23.    C          K = I1MACH(I)
   24.    C
   25.    C     where I=1,...,16.  The (output) value of K above is
   26.    C     determined by the (input) value of I.  The results for
   27.    C     various values of I are discussed below.
   28.    C
   29.    C  I/O unit numbers.
   30.    C    I1MACH( 1) = the standard input unit.
   31.    C    I1MACH( 2) = the standard output unit.
   32.    C    I1MACH( 3) = the standard punch unit.
   33.    C    I1MACH( 4) = the standard error message unit.
   34.    C
   35.    C  Words.
   36.    C    I1MACH( 5) = the number of bits per integer storage unit.
   37.    C    I1MACH( 6) = the number of characters per integer storage unit.
   38.    C
   39.    C  Integers.
   40.    C    assume integers are represented in the S-digit, base-A form
   41.    C
   42.    C               sign ( X(S-1)*A**(S-1) + ... + X(1)*A + X(0) )
   43.    C
   44.    C               where 0 .LE. X(I) .LT. A for I=0,...,S-1.
   45.    C    I1MACH( 7) = A, the base.
   46.    C    I1MACH( 8) = S, the number of base-A digits.
   47.    C    I1MACH( 9) = A**S - 1, the largest magnitude.
   48.    C
   49.    C  Floating-Point Numbers.
   50.    C    Assume floating-point numbers are represented in the T-digit,
   51.    C    base-B form
   52.    C               sign (B**E)*( (X(1)/B) + ... + (X(T)/B**T) )
   53.    C
   54.    C               where 0 .LE. X(I) .LT. B for I=1,...,T,
   55.    C               0 .LT. X(1), and EMIN .LE. E .LE. EMAX.
   56.    C    I1MACH(10) = B, the base.
   57.    C
   58.    C  Single-Precision
   59.    C    I1MACH(11) = T, the number of base-B digits.
   60.    C    I1MACH(12) = EMIN, the smallest exponent E.
   61.    C    I1MACH(13) = EMAX, the largest exponent E.
   62.    C
   63.    C  Double-Precision
   64.    C    I1MACH(14) = T, the number of base-B digits.
   65.    C    I1MACH(15) = EMIN, the smallest exponent E.
   66.    C    I1MACH(16) = EMAX, the largest exponent E.
   67.    C
   68.    C  To alter this function for a particular environment,
   69.    C  the desired set of DATA statements should be activated by
   70.    C  removing the C from column 1.  Also, the values of
   71.    C  I1MACH(1) - I1MACH(4) should be checked for consistency
   72.    C  with the local operating system.
   73.    C***REFERENCES  FOX P.A., HALL A.D., SCHRYER N.L.,*FRAMEWORK FOR A
   74.    C                 PORTABLE LIBRARY*, ACM TRANSACTIONS ON MATHEMATICAL
   75.    C                 SOFTWARE, VOL. 4, NO. 2, JUNE 1978, PP. 177-188.
   76.    C***ROUTINES CALLED  (NONE)
   77.    C***END PROLOGUE  I1MACH
   78.    C
   79.          INTEGER IMACH(16),OUTPUT
   80.          EQUIVALENCE (IMACH(4),OUTPUT)
